{
  "name": "1940_narvik",
  "title": "Battle of Narvik, April-June 1940",
  "notes": "Narvik, 13 Apr - 8 Jun 1940. The Allies' first land victory of the war. Roster at battalion level, names verified from en/fr/de/no/pl Wikipedia + Derry's official British history (Campaign in Norway, App. B + Ch. XIII). Attacker = the Allied Expeditionary Force under Gen. Be'thouart: 24th Guards Bde (1 Scots Gds / 1 Irish Gds / 2 SWB — present at Narvik Apr-16 May, then to the Bodo front; kept for the campaign-length battle), the 27e Demi-Brigade de Chasseurs Alpins (6e/12e/14e BCA — NOT the 27e BCA, which fought at the Ailette in France), the 13e Demi-Brigade de Legion Etrangere (1er/2e bns only, no III in 1940), the Polish Podhale Highland Bde (SBSP, FOUR battalions I-IV in two half-brigades + 25mm wz.34 AT guns — 'never seen mountains' per British reports, so standard mountaineer stats), and Fleischer's 6. Divisjon (II/IR 14, Lv.bat./IR 14, II/IR 15 — the 28 May assault battalion, I/IR 16, II/IR 16, and the elite Alta bataljon; I/IR 12 destroyed at Gratangen 25 Apr and I/IR 13 at Bjornfjell 16 Apr are pre-battle losses, I/IR 14 & I/IR 15 fought on Helgeland/Salten off-map). Supporting arms: 342e CACC (Hotchkiss H-39 tank company, ~10 tanks, 2 in the final assault), 31e Cie e'claireurs-skieurs ski company, 2e GAC 75mm batteries, 203rd Fd Bty (12x 25-pdr), Bergartilleribataljon nr. 3 + 10. motoriserte batteri, French AT coy, 404e RADCA AA. Defender = Gruppe Dietl (Gen. Dietl, 3. Gebirgs-Division, ~5,600 peak): the GJR 139 S-Staffel (~2,000, reinforced with Geb.Aufkl.112 elements), the SEVEN named Marine-Regiment Berger battalions of destroyer crews (Holtorf/Thiele/Zenker/Arnim/Erdmenger/Freytag-Loringhoven/Kothe, ~2,600, org 30 — ad-hoc infantry from the 10 sunk destroyers), FJR 1 paratroop company + GJR 138 companies air-landed via the frozen lakes (14 May-end, ~1,100 arrivals), I./GAR 112 mountain guns, and beached naval guns (2x 10.5cm FlaK + 11 light AA, sailor-manned). GJR 137 (2. Gebirgs-Division) did NOT reach Narvik during the battle (arrived symbolically 13 June) and is omitted. No German tanks/AT at Narvik. Attack axis = W (fjord side, province 3034): the Allies came from the Ofotfjord side (Harstad/Ballangen base area) against the town, while the German line of retreat ran east along the railway toward Bjornfjell/Sweden. Single W strip keeps the battle near the VP city — NE,SE strips (Bjerkvik side + railway corridor) would leave the city 50+ hexes from the contact line and freeze an AI-vs-AI battle at first contact (mountain standoff). Terrain: full mountain (3x move, -50% attack, LOS blocked) + fjord coastline; small-forces battle (real scale ~5k GER vs ~25k Allied). Stats = 1939 calibration; Allied bns full org 60, Norwegian mobilized org 50 (Alta elite 60, Lv. territorial 45), German naval personnel under-strength org 30, company-scale units (342e CACC/FJR 1 coy/GJR 138 coy/31e SES/AT/AA) org-discounted.",
